// DICOMWellKnownPalettes.swift
// DICOMCore
//
// DICOM Core — the eight Well-Known Color Palettes, transcribed from the
// standard.
//
// GENERATED FILE — the numbers below are DICOM PS3.6 Annex B, "Well-Known Color
// Palettes (Normative)", Tables B.1.N.2-2. Do not hand-edit: correct the
// transcription at its source and regenerate, or the palettes stop being the
// standard's.
//
// All eight carry the descriptor (256, 0, 8) — 256 entries, first value mapped
// 0, 8 bits per entry — and the standard specifies sRGB for each.
//
// Four of the eight (Spring, Summer, Fall, Winter) are published as *segmented*
// LUT data rather than explicit tables: short opcode streams in
// (0028,1221/1222/1223) that expand per PS3.3 C.7.9.2 (opcode 0 discrete,
// 1 linear, 2 indirect). They are expanded here at transcription time, so this
// file holds the resulting 256 entries and the renderers never need a decoder.
// Each expansion was checked to land on exactly 256 entries, which is the
// property a mis-read stream would almost certainly break.
//
// NEMA-verified: 2026a, checked 2026-09-24 — text-diffed: all 8 × 256 RGB entries match PS3.6
// 2026a Annex B (Tables B.1.N.2-2), with descriptors (256, 0, 8). The four segmented
// palettes were expanded per PS3.3 C.7.9.2. The standard gives no rounding rule for
// linear segments; these tables round half-to-even, which decides Summer[223].blue = 190
// (the exact value is 190.5).
//
// These are the only palettes a film can name normatively: PS3.3 Table C.13-5
// permits only RGB in a Basic Color Image Sequence, so a palette is always
// baked into the pixels before it reaches a printer. The identity survives in
// the audit trail, not on the wire.

